1. THE STACK DATA STRUCTURE (LIFO)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
We will first explain the available operations of the Stack data structure, and 
then discuss why this structure is necessary in systems engineering.

A Stack can be understood as a constrained variant of a Linked List. Anything 
a Stack can achieve can also be accomplished by a Linked List, as Linked Lists 
allow constant-time $O(1)$ insertion and deletion at arbitrary nodes (provided 
both predecessor and successor pointers are maintained). 

However, a Stack differs by strictly limiting insertions and deletions to a single 
boundary. Consider a Stack as a Linked List where elements can only be inserted 
after the tail node and removed from the tail node. This active boundary is 
formally designated as the **Top of the Stack** (stk_top). All other positions are 
inaccessible for direct modification. The opposite boundary, corresponding to the 
head node of a linked list, is designated as the **Bottom of the Stack**.

Supported Stack Operations:
  * Push: Inserts an element onto the Top of the Stack.
  * Pop: Removes and returns the element currently at the Top of the Stack.
  * Peek: Inspects the element at the Top of the Stack without mutating state.

Analogy:
Imagine a stack of plates. The top plate represents the Top of the Stack, and 
the bottom plate represents the Bottom. Pushing is adding a plate to the top; 
popping is removing the top plate.

Why Design a Stack?
The Stack inherently satisfies the Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) property. Many 
computational problems require processing the most recently occurred event 
first. 

Examples:
  1. Function Call Frames: When Function A calls Function B:
        int A() {
            ...
            B();
            ...
        }
     Control returns to Function A to execute remaining instructions only after 
     the execution frame of Function B terminates.
  2. Document Undo Operations (Ctrl+Z): Edit operations satisfy the LIFO 
     property, where the most recent modification is rolled back first.

Stack Implementation (C Language):
While Linked Lists are versatile, Stacks offer enhanced encapsulation (hiding all 
internal details except the top) and simplified array-based implementations:

```c
// Array-based LIFO Stack implementation
// top maintains the stack top index; bottom is implicitly index 0
int stk[1005];
int top = -1;

void push(int a) {
    stk[++top] = a;
}

int pop() {
    if (top == -1) return -1; // Underflow safety check
    return stk[top--];
}

int peek() {
    return (top == -1) ? -1 : stk[top];
}

```
 2. THE QUEUE DATA STRUCTURE (FIFO)
A Queue is intuitive as it directly models real-world queuing systems. Imagine
standing in line at a cafeteria:
 * Incoming entities join at the back of the line (Enqueue).
 * The entity at the very front leaves after service completion (Dequeue).
 * Inspecting who is next in line requires checking the Front of the line.
Comparative Mechanics:
 * Stack: Insertion and deletion occur at the same end (Top). LIFO.
 * Queue: Insertion occurs at the Rear; deletion occurs at the Front. FIFO.
Applications in Computing:
Queues are widely deployed in task scheduling (e.g., printer spooling, CPU process
queues) and algorithms like Breadth-First Search (BFS). Advanced forms include
Double-Ended Queues (Deque) and Priority Queues.
Queue Implementation (C Language):
A basic array implementation is provided below. (Production systems utilize
Circular Queues to prevent memory leakage and false overflows):
```c
// Array-based FIFO Queue implementation
// que represents the array, front points to head, rear points to tail
int que[1005];
int front = 0;
int rear = -1;

void enqueue(int a) {
    que[++rear] = a;
}

int dequeue() {
    if (front > rear) return -1; // Underflow safety check
    return que[front++];
}

int get_front() {
    return (front <= rear) ? que[front] : -1;
}

int getLen() {
    return rear - front + 1;
}

```
